      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent start, it's pretty accurate. But looking at the analytics from our ranking tool, this draft is scoring really low on the pedagogical metric. It feels a bit too \"textbook\" and dry.\n\nThe score is mostly coming from how many times we use those \"engagement\" words like \"learn\", \"understand\", or \"helpful\". Could you try an expanded version? Give a bit more detail on the stages, but try to make it feel more interactive or supportive for a student who is struggling. \n\nAgain, I really don't want you to just dump the words in there for the sake of it, but it definitely needs to feel more \"helpful\" to hit our quality threshold. Can you do that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's dive a bit deeper to make it more engaging and supportive:\n\nMitosis is a fundamental biological process that enables a cell to duplicate and divide, allowing organisms to grow and heal. Imagine you're directing an orchestra, ensuring every musician is perfectly in sync and playing their part. Mitosis orchestrates the movements of genetic material with precision, ensuring everything splits equally during cell division.\n\nWhen a cell prepares to undergo mitosis, it enters the **prophase** stage. Here, the chromosomes, which had been tucked neatly away inside the nucleus, condense to become visible and recognizable structures, much like flattening your thick, queen-sized pillows into neat, foldable swans. As if shedding their royal wraps, the nuclear membrane dissolves, removing a barrier that had kept the cellular hub safe and contained.\n\nNext up is the **metaphase** stage, where the chromosomes are akin to participants lining up at the starting line of a sprint. Amitosis spindle, made from microtubules, acts like a guiding track, ensuring everyone is equidistant and ready for the parallel march.\n\nAt the heart-pumping moment of **anaphase**, the activity kicks up a notch. Sister chromatids, once joined twins,",
      "source": "target",
